# Cloudflare Pages response headers for the frontend.
#
# This file is the other half of task 3.1.o. The Worker got the API security
# headers; these are the *page* headers, and they belong here rather than on the
# Worker because the Worker serves no HTML.
#
# Express used to serve the frontend itself (`express.static` at
# backend/src/index.ts:372, with an SPA `sendFile` fallback at :388), so the
# `helmet()` CSP at :73-90 covered these documents. When the frontend moved to
# Cloudflare Pages that coverage was lost silently -- no `_headers` file existed
# until this one, so the built app has been served with no CSP, no HSTS and no
# nosniff. Porting helmet's directives onto the Worker would not have restored
# it: a page policy on a JSON API governs nothing.
#
# Vite copies `publicDir` (this directory) into the build output, and
# `.github/workflows/pages-deploy.yml:122` deploys `frontend/build`, so this
# file ships with the app. Verified by building: `frontend/build/_headers`.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Frame-Options: DENY

# `camera=()` is deliberately ABSENT from Permissions-Policy.
#
# An earlier revision of this file included it, copied from the Worker's policy
# where it is correct (a JSON API needs no camera). On the frontend it is a
# critical bug: this is a barcode-scanning app. `/scan` is the post-login
# landing route (frontend/src/App.tsx:340, :419) and `CameraScanner.tsx:85-88`
# drives Quagga with `inputStream: { type: 'LiveStream', constraints:
# { facingMode: 'environment' } }`, i.e. getUserMedia. `camera=()` on the
# top-level document makes getUserMedia reject with NotAllowedError *without
# showing a prompt*, so every user would land in the scanner's error state.
#
# Unlike the CSP below, Permissions-Policy is enforced immediately -- it has no
# Report-Only mode -- so this would have broken the primary flow on deploy with
# no warning signal first. The other three directives are genuinely unused by
# the app and stay.
  Permissions-Policy: microphone=(), geolocation=(), browsing-topics=()

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CSP: deliberately Report-Only on this first deploy. Read before flipping.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every header above is safe to enforce immediately. A CSP is not, because its
# `connect-src` must name origins that cannot all be verified from this
# repository, and a miss does not degrade the app -- it breaks every API call
# and the whole sign-in flow.
#
# What IS known and already listed:
#   * https://api.expirymate.com.au -- the production API origin. Named in
#     .github/workflows/pages-deploy.yml:213, which fails the build if the
#     production Doppler config points REACT_APP_API_URL anywhere else.
#   * https://*.workers.dev -- preview deployments (pages-deploy.yml:93).
#   * The inline `Array.prototype.at` polyfill at frontend/index.html:5-14 is
#     allowed by its sha256 hash rather than by 'unsafe-inline'. Verified
#     against the BUILT file (frontend/build/index.html), not the source: Vite
#     copies it through byte-for-byte, so the hash is stable. If that polyfill
#     is ever edited the hash must be recomputed, or the script is blocked on
#     exactly the old browsers that need it (Safari <= 15.3, Chrome < 92).
#
# What is NOT known, and is the only reason this is still Report-Only:
#   * The Clerk frontend origin is derived from the publishable key at runtime.
#     The `*.clerk.accounts.dev` entry below covers development instances; a
#     production Clerk instance uses a custom domain (typically
#     clerk.<your-domain>) that must be added before enforcing.
#
# TO ENFORCE (once, after checking):
#   1. Load the deployed app, sign in, and exercise the scanner, an upload and
#      a report.
#   2. Collect every "Report Only" violation from the browser console -- each
#      names the origin it would have blocked.
#   3. Add those origins to the directives below.
#   4. Rename the header to `Content-Security-Policy` and redeploy.
#
# RECOMMENDED BEFORE STEP 1: add a reporting endpoint, so violations are
# collected from all real traffic rather than only from whoever runs the
# checklist by hand -- browsers, devices and the production Clerk domain that a
# single scripted session will not cover. Sentry accepts CSP reports at
#   https://<org-ingest-host>/api/<project-id>/security/?sentry_key=<public-key>
# which is derivable from REACT_APP_SENTRY_FRONTEND_DSN (that secret lives in
# Doppler, not this repo, which is why the directive is not filled in here).
# Append `; report-uri <that-url>` to the header below. It only changes where
# reports are sent, so it carries no risk of refusing a request.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-knCqSvgDjsOwNgTPb2+ztY1wa+3RkYnY20V/Hlkf9cY='; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com data:; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; connect-src 'self' https://api.expirymate.com.au https://*.workers.dev https://*.clerk.accounts.dev https://*.sentry.io; frame-src 'self' https://*.clerk.accounts.dev; worker-src 'self' blob:; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
